---
// <BcnSetupWorkspace> — MODEL A of two for Component setup: the DECISION WORKSPACE.
// A wide side drawer opened from the component dashboard's rail, in which a user
// decides which of the project's commitments apply to THIS component — in bulk or one
// at a time — and can see what each decision will DO before making it. The sibling
// model is <BcnSetupQueue>, which takes the same job as a triage queue; the two are
// alternatives, not layers, and only one will survive review.
//
// THE DATA-MODEL FINDING THIS REDESIGN TURNS ON. Prod has no "pending" state:
// ComponentCommitment.IsApplicable is a NOT NULL bit, so "pending" is manufactured by a
// view that LEFT JOINs every component in the project against every commitment in the
// project and calls the misses pending. The badge is therefore a CROSS PRODUCT — it
// counts the project, not the component — it re-inflates whenever any source document
// gains a commitment (a treadmill wearing a queue's clothing), and it counts the
// unfiltered set while the list beneath it is filtered to one source document. "52
// pending" over a list of three is the data model surfacing, not a rendering bug.
//
// So this drawer leads with three figures that are each scoped to this component and
// each openable as a list, and the view toggle's segment counts are COUNTED OFF THE
// RENDERED ROWS by the controller rather than read from a parallel total — the count and
// the list cannot disagree because they are the same act of counting.
//
// APPLYING HAS TEETH. Applying a commitment materializes its actions against the
// component (prod runs ReconcileImplementationsForApplicabilityChange on save), which is
// what fills the tracker; dismissing deletes the deletable ones back out. The preview
// pane therefore names the actions a decision will create, by code and name. Prod never
// says so, which is the single largest omission on the screen.
//
// NO CONSTRUCTION ACTIVITY FACET. Commitments are not associated to construction
// activities in the data model, which is why prod's filter is populated and permanently
// disabled. A control that cannot act is debt; polish is subtractive.
//
// OPENING IT. Anything in the page carrying [data-setup-workspace-open] opens the
// drawer — the same document-level delegate BcnGuidanceDrawer uses — so the component
// dashboard's rail needs no wiring of its own. The drawer emits `bcn-setup-decision`
// ({ ids, decision, rationale }) on every write and `bcn-setup-save`
// ({ applied, dismissed, count }) when the footer saves. One instance per page.
//
// VOCABULARY. "Actions", never "implementations". Never "AI", no persona, no sparkle
// glyph. A "Suggested" marker was here and was CUT at review (2026-08-13): it was
// authored into the fixture by hand, not derived, and Beacon has no signal that could
// produce it. Marking a row as recommended on the strength of nothing is worse than
// leaving the user to read the commitment.
//
// bcn-lego-checked: every control here is a lego — esa-side-dialog is the drawer,
// esa-stat the three lead figures, esa-progress-bar the decided share, esa-button-toggle
// the view pivot, esa-filter-container / esa-filter-dropdown /
// esa-filter-clear-button the facet bar (inside BcnFilterBar, the spoke's carded
// filter-bar idiom), esa-text-field the keyword search, esa-checkbox the row selector,
// esa-badge the decision / action-type chips, esa-icon-button + esa-tooltip the per-row
// decisions, esa-button every other action, esa-dialog + esa-textarea + esa-alert-box
// the rationale flow, esa-empty-state both nothing-here states, and BcnCommitmentBadge
// every commitment code. What is NOT a lego and is therefore hand-built here: (a) the
// SELECTABLE LIST ROW — Ecology has no selectable/expandable data row (esa-card is a
// container with no selection semantics or roving activation; esa-file-list is
// file-shaped; checked Beacon, whose equivalent is an AG Grid row, not portable to a
// 40%-wide drawer pane) so the row's hit area is a native <button>, which is also what
// makes it keyboard-operable; (b) the SELECTION ACTIONS in the list header — no esa-*
// selection/action bar exists (checked); and (c) this drawer's own two-pane geometry.
// bcn-setup-workspace is the reusable home for all three.
//
// THE PANES ARE A GRID, NOT THE .sidebar PRIMITIVE (review, 2026-08-13). .sidebar is a
// WRAPPING flex layout, and a wrapped flex line sizes to its tallest item's CONTENT —
// so both panes grew to full content height (2205px inside a 506px box), the inner
// scrollers never engaged, and the whole drawer scrolled as one: the figures, the view
// toggle and the filters all disappeared off the top while you worked the list. A
// two-column grid row is bounded by its container, which is what lets each pane scroll
// independently. Reach for .sidebar when content decides the height, not when the
// container does.
import EsaStat from '@esa/ecology/esa-stat.astro';
import EsaProgressBar from '@esa/ecology/esa-progress-bar.astro';
import EsaBadge from '@esa/ecology/esa-badge.astro';
import EsaIcon from '@esa/ecology/esa-icon.astro';
import EsaIconButton from '@esa/ecology/esa-icon-button.astro';
import EsaButton from '@esa/ecology/esa-button.astro';
import EsaEmptyState from '@esa/ecology/esa-empty-state.astro';
import EsaAlertBox from '@esa/ecology/esa-alert-box.astro';
import FilterClearButton from '@esa/ecology/esa-filter-clear-button.astro';
import BcnFilterBar from './BcnFilterBar.astro';
import BcnCommitmentBadge from './BcnCommitmentBadge.astro';
import BcnKeyValue from './BcnKeyValue.astro';
import {
  SETUP_COMMITMENTS,
  SETUP_TOTALS,
  DECIDED_PERCENT,
  LAST_REVIEWED,
  PENDING_ACTION_COUNT,
  SOURCE_BY_ID,
  SPECIES_LABEL,
  facetsFor,
  fmtDate,
} from '../../data/component-commitments';

interface Props {
  id?: string;
  componentName: string;
  /** Heading, default 'Component setup — applicable commitments'. */
  heading?: string;
}
const {
  id = 'bcn-setup-workspace',
  componentName,
  heading = 'Applicable Commitments',
} = Astro.props;

/** Not in esa-icon's registry; passed through its `paths` escape hatch. */
const CLIPBOARD_CHECK =
  '<rect width="8" height="4" x="8" y="2" rx="1" ry="1"/><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"/><path d="m9 14 2 2 4-4"/>';

/** Action-type words as the tracker says them. Local so the fixture stays the only import. */
const ACTION_TYPE_LABEL: Record<string, string> = {
  tracking: 'Tracking',
  monitoring: 'Monitoring',
  reporting: 'Reporting',
};

const plural = (n: number, one: string, many = `${one}s`) => (n === 1 ? one : many);

/** Undecided AND added after the last decision here — the treadmill, made legible. */
const isNew = (addedOn: string) => addedOn > LAST_REVIEWED;

/** The quiet meta line under each row title. */
const rowMeta = (c: (typeof SETUP_COMMITMENTS)[number]) =>
  [
    SOURCE_BY_ID[c.sourceId]?.short,
    c.requirementType,
    c.species.length ? c.species.join(', ') : null,
    c.actions.length ? `${c.actions.length} ${plural(c.actions.length, 'action')}` : 'No actions',
  ]
    .filter(Boolean)
    .join(' · ');
---

<esa-side-dialog
  id={id}
  class="bcn-sw"
  size="lg"
  position="right"
  heading={heading}
  data-setup-workspace
>
  {/* The workflow's icon, its name, and the record it acts on as a BADGE — the
      title-plus-badge pattern Beacon already uses (PageLayout's `title-badge` slot).
      "Component setup" is gone: it was the tab's name, not this dialog's job, and the
      component's name reads better beside the title than as a line under it. */}
  <div slot="header" class="bcn-sw__head">
    <EsaIcon name="clipboard-check" paths={CLIPBOARD_CHECK} size="lg" />
    <h2 class="bcn-sw__headtitle">{heading}</h2>
    <EsaBadge value={componentName} variant="secondary" size="sm" />
  </div>

  <div class="bcn-sw__body">
    {/* Lead band — the honest counts. Each figure is scoped to this component and each
        one opens as a list below. The sub-lines under them are gone: a label and a
        number are the statement, and "23 actions they would create" was narration
        about a figure sitting directly above it. */}
    <header class="bcn-sw__lead">
      <div class="bcn-sw__figures">
        <div class="bcn-sw__figure" data-sw-figure="undecided">
          <EsaStat value={SETUP_TOTALS.undecided} label="Need a decision" />
        </div>
        <div class="bcn-sw__figure" data-sw-figure="new">
          <EsaStat value={SETUP_TOTALS.newSince} label="New since last review" />
        </div>
        <div class="bcn-sw__figure bcn-sw__figure--progress" data-sw-figure="decided">
          <EsaStat value={`${SETUP_TOTALS.decided} of ${SETUP_TOTALS.total}`} label="Decided" />
          <div class="bcn-sw__bar" data-sw-progress>
            <EsaProgressBar value={DECIDED_PERCENT} size="sm" showValue label="" />
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- ── 2 + 3. View toggle and the facets it recomputes. Segment counts are counted
         off the rendered rows by the controller, so a segment can never claim a number
         the list beneath it does not produce. ── -->
    <div class="bcn-sw__controls">
      <BcnFilterBar filtersLabel="Filters">
        {/* The bar's lead slot is exactly what it was extracted for: a View pivot. The
            toggle carries its own `label` so the lego wires aria-labelledby onto its
            radiogroup — a leadLabel here would only duplicate it.
            size="md", not "sm": at sm the lego resolves --form-font-size-sm (12px) for
            BOTH its label and its segment text, and these segments are this drawer's
            primary navigation — 12px is under the house floor. md lands at 15px / 36px.
            It sits in the bar's TOP band, so it has no same-row sibling to match. */}
        <esa-button-toggle slot="lead" data-sw-view size="md" label="View"></esa-button-toggle>
        {/* Keyword search beside the facets, the same control every other data surface
            in this spoke uses (the grid kit's search, BcnWorkAreaBoard's). It matches
            the commitment code, its title, and its text. */}
        <span class="bcn-sw__search">
          <esa-text-field data-sw-search size="sm" placeholder="Search commitments…"></esa-text-field>
        </span>
        <esa-filter-dropdown data-sw-facet="source" name="source" label="Source document" multiple size="sm"></esa-filter-dropdown>
        <esa-filter-dropdown data-sw-facet="type" name="type" label="Requirement type" multiple size="sm"></esa-filter-dropdown>
        <esa-filter-dropdown data-sw-facet="species" name="species" label="Species" multiple size="sm"></esa-filter-dropdown>
        {/* No active-filter pills: each dropdown already shows its own selection, so
            the chips restated a state the control beside them was displaying. */}
        <span slot="actions" data-sw-clear><FilterClearButton /></span>
      </BcnFilterBar>
    </div>

    <!-- ── 4. Two panes: the list decides, the preview explains. .sidebar primitive —
         list ~40%, preview the rest. ── -->
    <div class="bcn-sw__panes">
      <section class="bcn-sw__pane bcn-sw__pane--list" aria-label="Commitments in view">
        {/* List header — the home for select-all, which the first pass had nowhere
            to live, so the checkboxes offered no way to take a whole filtered set.
            Selecting all selects what is VISIBLE, never the hidden remainder: a
            control that silently reaches past the filter you just set is a trap. */}
        {/* List header — STATIC at the top of the column, so select-all and the bulk
            actions are reachable without scrolling to find them. The bulk bar used to
            float at the bottom of the list, which meant selecting a row near the top
            and then hunting for the buttons. Everything about the selection now lives
            in one place, next to the control that makes a selection. */}
        <div class="bcn-sw__listhead">
          <esa-checkbox size="sm" data-sw-check-all aria-label="Select every commitment in view"></esa-checkbox>
          <span class="bcn-sw__listcount" data-sw-listcount></span>
          <span class="bcn-sw__listacts" data-sw-bulk hidden>
            <EsaButton size="sm" color="primary" data-sw-bulk-apply>Apply</EsaButton>
            <EsaButton size="sm" color="ghost" appearance="outline" data-sw-bulk-dismiss>Dismiss</EsaButton>
            <EsaButton size="sm" color="ghost" data-sw-bulk-clear>Clear</EsaButton>
          </span>
        </div>

        <div class="bcn-sw__scroll" data-sw-scroll>
          <ul class="bcn-sw__rows" data-sw-rows>
            {
              SETUP_COMMITMENTS.map((c) => (
                <li
                  class="bcn-sw-row"
                  data-sw-row={c.id}
                  data-code={c.code}
                  data-decision={c.decision ?? ''}
                  data-new={isNew(c.addedOn) ? 'true' : 'false'}
                  data-source={c.sourceId}
                  data-type={c.requirementType}
                  data-species={c.species.join(' ')}
                  data-rationale={c.rationale ? 'true' : 'false'}
                  hidden
                >
                  {/* The checkbox owns a full-height GUTTER of its own (review,
                      2026-08-13). Nothing else may enter that column — the meta line and
                      the action buttons used to run underneath it, which buried the one
                      control that starts a bulk decision. */}
                  <span class="bcn-sw-row__check">
                    <esa-checkbox
                      size="sm"
                      data-sw-check={c.id}
                      aria-label={`Select ${c.code} for a bulk decision`}
                    />
                  </span>

                  <span class="bcn-sw-row__content">
                    <span class="bcn-sw-row__top">
                      <button
                        type="button"
                        class="bcn-sw-row__hit"
                        data-sw-open={c.id}
                        aria-label={`Show ${c.code} ${c.title} in the preview`}
                      >
                        <span class="bcn-sw-row__titleline">
                          <BcnCommitmentBadge code={c.code} size="sm" />
                          <span class="bcn-sw-row__name" title={c.title}>{c.title}</span>
                        </span>
                      </button>

                      {/* The decision, as a chip — never a row edge. */}
                      <span class="bcn-sw-row__marks">
                        <span class="bcn-sw-row__state bcn-sw-row__state--applied">
                          <EsaBadge value="Applied" size="sm" />
                        </span>
                        <span class="bcn-sw-row__state bcn-sw-row__state--dismissed">
                          <EsaBadge value="Dismissed" size="sm" />
                        </span>
                      </span>
                    </span>

                    <span class="bcn-sw-row__meta">{rowMeta(c)}</span>

                    {/* Icon buttons with tooltips rather than three text buttons: the
                        words repeated on every row for a decision that is the same on
                        every row. The tooltip carries the verb, and the icon-button's
                        `label` is its accessible name, so nothing is lost to a screen
                        reader. Always laid out, only visibility changes, so a row is
                        exactly as tall hovered as not. */}
                    <span class="bcn-sw-row__acts">
                      <esa-tooltip text="Apply to this component" position="top">
                        <span data-sw-apply={c.id}>
                          <EsaIconButton icon="check" label={`Apply ${c.code}`} size="sm" />
                        </span>
                      </esa-tooltip>
                      <esa-tooltip text="Not applicable" position="top">
                        <span data-sw-dismiss={c.id}>
                          <EsaIconButton icon="x" label={`Dismiss ${c.code}`} size="sm" />
                        </span>
                      </esa-tooltip>
                      <span class="bcn-sw-row__undo">
                        <esa-tooltip text="Undo this decision" position="top">
                          <span data-sw-undo={c.id}>
                            <EsaIconButton icon="rotate-ccw" label={`Undo ${c.code}`} size="sm" />
                          </span>
                        </esa-tooltip>
                      </span>
                    </span>
                  </span>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ul>

          <div class="bcn-sw__none" data-sw-none hidden>
            <EsaEmptyState title="No commitments match these filters" size="sm" />
          </div>
        </div>

      </section>

      <section class="bcn-sw__pane bcn-sw__pane--preview" aria-label="Selected commitment">
        <div class="bcn-sw__scroll bcn-sw__scroll--pad">
          <div class="bcn-sw__nopreview" data-sw-nopreview>
            <EsaEmptyState title="Select a commitment" size="sm" />
          </div>

          {
            SETUP_COMMITMENTS.map((c) => (
              <article class="bcn-sw-prev" data-sw-preview={c.id} hidden>
                <header class="bcn-sw-prev__head">
                  <BcnCommitmentBadge code={c.code} />
                  <h3 class="bcn-sw-prev__title">{c.title}</h3>
                </header>

                {/* The document voice — the commitment's own language, quoted. */}
                <p class="bcn-sw-prev__doc">{c.text}</p>

                <div class="bcn-sw-prev__facts">
                  <BcnKeyValue label="Source document" value={SOURCE_BY_ID[c.sourceId]?.name} />
                  <BcnKeyValue label="Requirement type" value={c.requirementType} />
                  <BcnKeyValue
                    label="Requirements"
                    value={`${c.requirements} ${plural(c.requirements, 'requirement')}`}
                  />
                  <BcnKeyValue label="Species">
                    <span class="bcn-sw-prev__species">
                      {c.species.length
                        ? c.species.map((s) => `${s} · ${SPECIES_LABEL[s]}`).join(', ')
                        : 'None'}
                    </span>
                  </BcnKeyValue>
                  <BcnKeyValue label="Added to the project" value={fmtDate(c.addedOn)} />
                </div>

                {/* THE PART PROD OMITS: what the decision DOES. Slimmed at review
                    (2026-08-13) from a boxed panel to a labelled section, because the
                    box gave a one-action commitment the visual weight of a warning.
                    It is NOT a restatement of the facts above it: those count
                    REQUIREMENTS, this names the ACTIONS that get materialised onto the
                    component — a different entity, sometimes several, sometimes none,
                    each landing in a different module. That relationship is the whole
                    reason this screen has consequences, and prod never states it. */}
                <section class="bcn-sw-prev__effect">
                  <h4 class="bcn-sw-prev__efftitle">
                    {c.actions.length
                      ? `Creates ${c.actions.length} ${plural(c.actions.length, 'action')} on this component`
                      : 'Creates no actions on this component'}
                  </h4>
                  {c.actions.length > 0 && (
                    <ul class="bcn-sw-prev__actions">
                      {c.actions.map((a) => (
                        <li class="bcn-sw-prev__action">
                          <BcnCommitmentBadge code={a.code} size="sm" />
                          <span class="bcn-sw-prev__actname">{a.name}</span>
                          {/* The module the created action lands in — Tracking /
                              Monitoring / Reporting. Derived in prod from the action's
                              RequirementType; that mapping is still an open ADR on
                              BCN-1041, so treat the label as illustrative. */}
                          <span class="bcn-sw-prev__acttype">
                            <EsaBadge value={ACTION_TYPE_LABEL[a.type] ?? a.type} size="sm" />
                          </span>
                        </li>
                      ))}
                    </ul>
                  )}
                </section>

                {/* Decision + rationale on record. Toggled by the controller. */}
                <section class="bcn-sw-prev__decision" data-sw-prev-decision hidden>
                  <BcnKeyValue label="Decision">
                    <span class="bcn-sw-prev__decval" data-sw-prev-decval />
                  </BcnKeyValue>
                  <BcnKeyValue label="Rationale">
                    <span class="bcn-sw-prev__rationale" data-sw-prev-rationale>
                      {c.rationale ?? ''}
                    </span>
                  </BcnKeyValue>
                </section>
              </article>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ── 6. Footer — Windows order: the tertiary state far left, the action group right
       with the primary LEFT of Cancel. ── -->
  <div slot="footer" class="bcn-sw__foot">
    <span class="bcn-sw__pending" data-sw-pending hidden></span>
    <span class="bcn-sw__footacts">
      <EsaButton color="primary" data-sw-save>Save decisions</EsaButton>
      <EsaButton color="ghost" appearance="outline" data-sw-cancel>Cancel</EsaButton>
    </span>
  </div>
</esa-side-dialog>

<!-- ── The rationale dialog for a BULK write. The overwrite warning is the point: in
     prod a batch decision silently replaces a rationale someone already wrote, and the
     count of what is about to be lost is never shown. ── -->
<esa-dialog id={`${id}-rationale`} class="bcn-sw-rationale" heading="Apply commitments" data-sw-dialog>
  <div class="bcn-sw-rationale__body">
    <p class="bcn-sw-rationale__what type-body" data-sw-dialog-what></p>

    <div class="bcn-sw-rationale__warn" data-sw-dialog-warn hidden>
      <EsaAlertBox variant="warning" title="This replaces a rationale already on record">
        <span data-sw-dialog-warn-text></span>
      </EsaAlertBox>
    </div>

    <esa-textarea
      data-sw-dialog-rationale
      label="Rationale"
      rows="4"
      placeholder="Rationale"
    ></esa-textarea>
  </div>

  <div slot="footer" class="bcn-sw-rationale__foot">
    <EsaButton color="primary" data-sw-dialog-confirm>Apply</EsaButton>
    <EsaButton color="ghost" appearance="outline" data-sw-dialog-cancel>Cancel</EsaButton>
  </div>
</esa-dialog>

<script>
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-side-dialog';
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-dialog';
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-button-toggle';
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-filter-dropdown';
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-checkbox';
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-text-field';
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-tooltip';
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-textarea';
  import { initSetupWorkspace } from './setup-workspace';
  initSetupWorkspace();
</script>

<style>
  /* ── Drawer geometry. FLUSH to the right edge, full height (review, 2026-08-13):
       this first shipped inset 7vh top and bottom, chasing the ~86vh stable-footprint
       rule, which made it read as a floating panel. Every other drawer in Beacon comes
       in flush against the edge, and approved patterns are canon — an inset drawer here
       would be a new treatment introduced by accident rather than on purpose. The
       stability the rule is actually protecting is preserved: the frame is a fixed
       width and full height, and the panes scroll INSIDE it, so nothing moves when the
       user filters or selects. Wide, because this is a work surface, not a slim rail. ── */
  .bcn-sw {
    --side-dialog-width-lg: 1180px;
    --z-modal-backdrop: 1300;
    --z-modal: 1301;
    --backdrop-filter: blur(2px);
    /* Every small CONTROL in this drawer reads 14px, not 12px. --form-font-size-sm
       resolves to --type-size-100 (12px), which is the right density for a chip but
       under the house floor for something you click — the inline Apply / Dismiss and
       the bulk bar are actions, not meta. Re-pointed once here so a control never
       disagrees with its neighbour, rather than patched per button. */
    --form-font-size-sm: var(--type-size-150);
  }

  /* The lego's .body is a definite-height flex child, so height:100% here fills it
     exactly and the panes below own all the scrolling. */
  .bcn-sw__body {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--spacing-400);
    height: 100%;
    min-height: 0;
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
  }

  /* Header slot: workflow glyph, name, and the record it acts on as a badge. */
  .bcn-sw__head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--spacing-300);
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .bcn-sw__headtitle {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--type-size-400);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .bcn-sw__head :global(.esa-icon) { color: var(--color-text-secondary); }
  /* The record badge is a LABEL, not a status: quiet hairline and text, per the house
     badge standard. esa-badge's variants are all saturated FILLS, which put a block of
     brand colour in the title bar and made the component's name read as an alert.
     Overridden with `background`/`color` rather than the --badge-* tokens: the variant
     class sets those properties directly, so re-pointing the token does nothing. */
  .bcn-sw__head :global(.esa-badge) {
    background: transparent;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
  }

  .bcn-sw__search {
    display: inline-flex;
    min-width: 14rem;
  }
  .bcn-sw__search :global(esa-text-field) { width: 100%; }

  /* ── Lead band ── */
  .bcn-sw__lead {
    flex: none;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--spacing-300);
  }
  .bcn-sw__scope {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
  }
  .bcn-sw__figures {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: var(--spacing-700);
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }
  .bcn-sw__figure {
    min-width: 0;
    /* One step down from the lego default: three figures on one band, and the drawer is
       a work surface, not a dashboard hero. */
    --stat-value-size: var(--type-size-600);
  }
  .bcn-sw__figure--progress {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--spacing-200);
    min-width: 220px;
  }
  .bcn-sw__bar {
    max-width: 260px;
  }

  /* ── Controls ── */
  .bcn-sw__controls {
    flex: none;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--spacing-300);
  }
  /* The pill row rides inside the filter container. Every possible chip is pre-rendered
     (esa-filter-pills is compile-time) and the controller reveals the active ones; the
     wrapper itself hides when none are, so an empty bar has no stray gap.
     Neutral chips: --color-primary-bg is not a Beacon token, so the lego's default pill
     fill would resolve to its literal green fallback. Re-point to the house neutral pair. */
  .bcn-sw__pills {
    display: inline-flex;
    min-width: 0;
    --filter-pill-bg: var(--color-surface-sunken);
    --filter-pill-bg-hover: var(--color-border);
    --filter-pill-text: var(--color-text-primary);
  }
  .bcn-sw__pills[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }
  .bcn-sw__pills :global(.esa-filter-pills__chip[hidden]) {
    display: none;
  }

  /* ── The two panes ── */
  /* A GRID, deliberately not the .sidebar primitive. .sidebar is a WRAPPING flex
     layout, and a wrapped flex line sizes to its tallest item's content — so the two
     panes grew to their full content height (2205px inside a 506px box), the inner
     scrollers never engaged, and the whole drawer scrolled as one: filters and stats
     disappeared off the top while you worked the list. A two-column grid row is
     bounded by the container, which is what lets each pane scroll on its own. */
  .bcn-sw__panes {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 2fr) minmax(0, 3fr);
    gap: var(--spacing-400);
    flex: 1;
    min-height: 0;
    align-items: stretch;
  }
  .bcn-sw__pane {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    min-height: 0;
    min-width: 0;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-200);
    background: var(--color-surface);
  }
  .bcn-sw__pane--list {
    position: relative;
    background: var(--color-surface-sunken);
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .bcn-sw__scroll {
    flex: 1;
    min-height: 0;
    overflow-y: auto;
    padding: var(--spacing-300);
  }
  .bcn-sw__scroll--pad {
    padding: var(--spacing-500);
  }
  .bcn-sw__rows {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--spacing-200);
  }
  .bcn-sw__none[hidden],
  .bcn-sw__nopreview[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  /* ── A row ── */
  /* SLIM. Twenty-six of these stack in a 40% pane, so a row is two FIXED lines — a
     title and a meta line, each clipped rather than wrapped — over the action band.
     Wrapping is what made the cards ragged and tall; the full title is a hover away
     (title attribute) or a click away (the preview). */
  /* TWO COLUMNS: a gutter the checkbox owns outright, and everything else. Nothing
     may enter that gutter — the meta line and the buttons used to run underneath the
     checkbox, burying the one control that starts a bulk decision. */
  .bcn-sw-row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: auto minmax(0, 1fr);
    align-items: start;
    column-gap: var(--spacing-400);
    padding: var(--spacing-250, 0.625rem) var(--spacing-300);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-200);
    background: var(--color-surface);
    transition:
      border-color 120ms ease,
      background 120ms ease;
  }
  .bcn-sw-row[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }
  .bcn-sw-row__content {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--spacing-100);
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .bcn-sw-row__top {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--spacing-250, 0.625rem);
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .bcn-sw-row__marks {
    flex: none;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--spacing-150);
  }
  .bcn-sw-row:hover {
    border-color: var(--color-border-strong);
  }
  /* Being previewed. A tint plus a full border — never a coloured left edge. */
  .bcn-sw-row[data-active] {
    border-color: var(--color-primary);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-primary) 5%, var(--color-surface));
  }
  /* Vertically centred on the title line, not the whole card, so it reads as
     belonging to the row rather than floating in the gutter. */
  .bcn-sw-row__check {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 1.5rem;
  }
  /* The row's hit area is a real button — keyboard-operable, and it announces the row it
     opens. It carries no button chrome: the ROW is the object, this is its surface. */
  .bcn-sw-row__hit {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 2px;
    padding: 0;
    border: 0;
    background: none;
    font: inherit;
    text-align: left;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .bcn-sw-row__hit:focus-visible {
    outline: var(--focus-ring-width) solid var(--focus-ring-color);
    outline-offset: 2px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-100);
  }
  /* nowrap + one line each: the row's height is now a constant, so a filtered list
     reads as an even stack rather than a ragged one. */
  .bcn-sw-row__titleline {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    flex-wrap: nowrap;
    gap: var(--spacing-200);
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .bcn-sw-row__name {
    min-width: 0;
    /* 14px — the dense-surface base. This is a scan-and-pick list, not prose. */
    font-size: var(--type-size-150);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    line-height: 1.35;
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
    overflow: hidden;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .bcn-sw-row__meta {
    /* 13px is the deliberate floor: the scale steps 14px straight to 12px, and 12px is
       under the house minimum. Colour steps UP as size steps down — darken as you
       shrink, never grey out. */
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    overflow: hidden;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  /* The suggestion marker: a quiet 4px badge, tinted away from the entity ramp so it
     cannot be mistaken for one. Same treatment as the evidence drawer's. */
  .bcn-sw-row__mark :global(.esa-badge) {
    --badge-bg: var(--color-info-subtle);
    --badge-text-color: var(--color-text-primary);
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-info) 35%, transparent);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
  }
  /* Decision chips — shown by the row's own data-decision, no JS toggling needed. */
  .bcn-sw-row__state {
    flex: none;
    display: none;
  }
  .bcn-sw-row[data-decision='applied'] .bcn-sw-row__state--applied,
  .bcn-sw-row[data-decision='dismissed'] .bcn-sw-row__state--dismissed {
    display: inline-flex;
  }
  /* Quiet chips in the house style — a hairline and text, no saturated fill. The
     first pass used a bright success green that belongs to no Beacon ramp and read
     as a status signal; Applied is a FACT about a decision, not an alarm, so it
     wears the brand teal as text on the plain surface. */
  .bcn-sw-row__state--applied :global(.esa-badge) {
    --badge-bg: transparent;
    --badge-text-color: var(--color-primary);
    --badge-border-color: var(--color-primary-border, var(--color-border));
    border: 1px solid var(--color-primary-border, var(--color-border));
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
  }
  .bcn-sw-row__state--dismissed :global(.esa-badge) {
    --badge-bg: transparent;
    --badge-text-color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
  }
  /* Inline actions: revealed on hover or when anything inside has focus, so keyboard
     users reach them too. Space is reserved by visibility, not display — the row's width
     does not jump as the pointer crosses it. */
  /* ALWAYS laid out, only visibility changes, so a row is exactly as tall hovered as
     not. `visibility` also keeps the buttons out of the tab order while hidden, and
     :focus-within reveals them for the keyboard path. */
  .bcn-sw-row__acts {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--spacing-150);
    visibility: hidden;
  }
  .bcn-sw-row:hover .bcn-sw-row__acts,
  .bcn-sw-row:focus-within .bcn-sw-row__acts {
    visibility: visible;
  }
  /* Undo appears only once this row carries a staged decision — the way back out of
     one without hunting for it in a bulk bar. It is an esa-button like its two
     siblings rather than an underlined link: a text link sitting in a row of buttons
     read as a different KIND of thing when it is the same kind of thing. */
  .bcn-sw-row__undo {
    margin-left: auto;
    display: none;
  }
  .bcn-sw-row[data-staged='true'] .bcn-sw-row__undo {
    display: inline-flex;
  }
  /* A staged row keeps its buttons visible, so the decision reads as reversible
     rather than as something that already happened behind your back. */
  .bcn-sw-row[data-staged='true'] .bcn-sw-row__acts {
    visibility: visible;
  }

  /* List header — static at the top of the column: select-all, what is in view, and
     the bulk actions. `flex: none` keeps it out of the scroller below it, so it is
     always on screen no matter how far down the list you are. */
  .bcn-sw__listhead {
    flex: none;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--spacing-250, 0.625rem);
    min-height: 40px;
    padding: var(--spacing-200) var(--spacing-300);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border-light);
  }
  .bcn-sw__listcount {
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .bcn-sw__listacts {
    margin-left: auto;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--spacing-150);
  }
  .bcn-sw__listacts[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  /* ── The preview ── */
  .bcn-sw-prev {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--spacing-400);
  }
  .bcn-sw-prev[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }
  .bcn-sw-prev__head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--spacing-300);
  }
  .bcn-sw-prev__title {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--type-size-400);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    line-height: 1.25;
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
  }
  /* The document voice: Beacon's designated serif (Besley) on the citation surface, the
     same treatment the commitment detail page gives quoted regulatory language. */
  .bcn-sw-prev__doc {
    margin: 0;
    padding: var(--spacing-400);
    border-radius: var(--radius-200);
    background: var(--bcn-citation-bg);
    font-family: var(--font-decorative);
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
  }
  .bcn-sw-prev__facts {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr));
    gap: var(--spacing-300) var(--spacing-500);
  }
  .bcn-sw-prev__species {
    font-size: var(--form-font-size-md);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
  }

  /* A divided section, not a boxed panel. The box read as a warning, which is the
     wrong weight for "this creates one action" — the consequence is worth stating,
     not alarming about. */
  .bcn-sw-prev__effect {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--spacing-250, 0.625rem);
    padding-top: var(--spacing-300);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border-light);
  }
  .bcn-sw-prev__efftitle {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--type-size-200);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
  }
  .bcn-sw-prev__actions {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--spacing-200);
  }
  .bcn-sw-prev__action {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--spacing-200);
    padding: var(--spacing-200) var(--spacing-300);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-100);
    background: var(--color-surface);
  }
  .bcn-sw-prev__actname {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    font-size: var(--type-size-150);
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
  }
  .bcn-sw-prev__acttype :global(.esa-badge) {
    --badge-bg: var(--color-surface-sunken);
    --badge-text-color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
  }
  .bcn-sw-prev__undo {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
  }

  .bcn-sw-prev__decision {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--spacing-300) var(--spacing-500);
    padding-top: var(--spacing-300);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border-light);
  }
  .bcn-sw-prev__decision[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }
  .bcn-sw-prev__decval,
  .bcn-sw-prev__rationale {
    font-size: var(--form-font-size-md);
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
  }

  /* ── Footer ── */
  .bcn-sw__foot {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--spacing-400);
    width: 100%;
  }
  .bcn-sw__pending {
    font-size: var(--type-size-150);
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
  }
  .bcn-sw__pending[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }
  .bcn-sw__footacts {
    margin-left: auto;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--spacing-300);
  }

  /* ── Rationale dialog ── */
  .bcn-sw-rationale {
    --z-modal-backdrop: 1400;
    --z-modal: 1401;
  }
  .bcn-sw-rationale__body {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--spacing-400);
  }
  .bcn-sw-rationale__what {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
  }
  .bcn-sw-rationale__warn[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }
  .bcn-sw-rationale__foot {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: flex-end;
    gap: var(--spacing-300);
    width: 100%;
  }

  /* Narrow: the panes stack rather than crushing the list. */
  @media (max-width: 60rem) {
    .bcn-sw__panes {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      grid-template-rows: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }
</style>
